D={
"pallava-dynasty":("Pallava society centred on Kanchipuram, a city of temples and learning whose ghatika (college) drew students from across India; Xuanzang visited in the 7th century and found over 100 Buddhist monasteries alongside Hindu temples. Farmers in the Palar valley grew rice with tank irrigation, and village assemblies (sabha and ur) managed local affairs. The port of Mamallapuram linked Tamil merchants to Sri Lanka and Southeast Asia, carrying Pallava script and temple styles abroad.","Pallava kings were patrons of Shaivism and Vaishnavism during the rise of the Tamil Bhakti movement: the Nayanar poets (Appar, Sambandar) and Alvars (Andal, Nammalvar) sang devotional hymns in Tamil, challenging Buddhist and Jain influence. Mahendravarman I wrote the Sanskrit satire Mattavilasa Prahasana. The Mamallapuram reliefs, Shore Temple and Kailasanatha temple mark the birth of Dravidian architecture."),
"chalukya-dynasty":("The Badami Chalukyas ruled an agrarian Deccan society of farming villages that grew millets, rice and cotton and paid taxes to the king's feudatories. Aihole was home to the Ainnurruvar, a powerful guild of '500 merchants' who traded across south India and overseas. Kings granted land to Brahmins and temples, which became centres of village life.","Chalukya patronage produced an extraordinary laboratory of temple styles at Aihole, Badami and Pattadakal, mixing northern nagara and southern dravida forms; cave temples at Badami are carved with Shiva, Vishnu and Jain tirthankaras. Kannada inscriptions appear alongside Sanskrit, and the Jain poet Ravikirti composed the Aihole inscription in Sanskrit verse."),
"rashtrakuta-dynasty":("The Rashtrakuta capital Manyakheta was a wealthy city, and Arab travellers like Sulaiman and al-Masudi described the kingdom as one of the world's four great powers, friendly to Muslim merchants on the west coast. Agriculture, cotton textiles and coastal trade sustained the economy, with feudatories collecting revenue.","The Rashtrakutas patronised Shaivism, Vaishnavism and especially Jainism; Amoghavarsha I is said to have become a Jain monk. They sponsored Kannada classics such as Kavirajamarga and Adikavi Pampa's works, Sanskrit mathematics (Mahavira's Ganitasarasangraha), and the monolithic Kailasa temple at Ellora, where Hindu, Buddhist and Jain caves stand side by side."),
"pala-empire":("Pala Bengal and Bihar were rich rice-growing lands with river trade to the Bay of Bengal and Southeast Asia. Land grants inscribed on copper plates record villages, ponds, fields and boundaries, giving a picture of rural life; many villagers were farmers, fishermen and weavers.","The Palas were the last great royal patrons of Buddhism in India, supporting Nalanda and founding Vikramashila and Somapura. Scholars such as Atisha Dipankara travelled to Tibet, shaping Tibetan Buddhism. Pala bronzes and palm-leaf manuscript paintings influenced Nepal, Tibet and Southeast Asia, and Bengali language began to emerge in the Charyapada songs."),
"ahom-kingdom":("Under the paik system, every adult man was a paik who served the state for part of the year — as soldier, labourer or craftsman — in return for land; groups of paiks (khels) were managed by officers. Farmers grew wet rice in the Brahmaputra valley, fished and raised silk (muga and eri); villages managed embankments and roads.","Ahom kings arrived as Tai Buddhists and animists, gradually adopting Hinduism and Assamese culture, while keeping Tai rituals. They wrote detailed court chronicles called Buranjis, first in Tai Ahom and later in Assamese — rare historical records in South Asia. Bihu festivals and Sattriya dance, from Sankardev's Vaishnava monasteries (sattras), flourished."),
"maratha-confederacy":("Most Marathas were peasant farmers of the Deccan plateau who also served as cavalry. Shivaji paid soldiers in cash, regulated revenue and protected peasants from plunder by his own troops. Later the Peshwas' Pune was a growing city of bankers, merchants and Brahmin administrators, while armies raised chauth across India.","Maratha identity drew on the Bhakti tradition of saints like Dnyaneshwar, Namdev, Eknath and Tukaram, whose abhangs are sung on the annual wari pilgrimage to Pandharpur. Shivaji used Marathi in administration and created the Rajavyavahara Kosha, a dictionary to replace Persian terms. Powadas (ballads) celebrated warriors."),
"sikh-empire":("Ranjit Singh's Punjab was a fertile farming land; he reduced oppressive taxes in some areas and encouraged trade, and Lahore and Amritsar became busy commercial centres. His army recruited Sikhs, Hindus, Muslims and even European officers, providing many jobs.","The empire was rooted in Sikh teachings of one God, equality and service, expressed in langar (community kitchen) and the Khalsa. Ranjit Singh gilded the Harmandir Sahib, but also gave to Hindu temples and mosques. Punjabi poetry, such as Waris Shah's Heer (1766), and miniature painting from the hill states flourished."),
"kingdom-of-mysore":("Mysore's farmers grew ragi, rice, sugarcane and coconut, irrigated by tanks and the Kaveri; Tipu Sultan promoted sericulture, introducing silkworms from China, and built the foundation of the Kannambadi dam. Sandalwood was a royal monopoly. Tipu's state also built factories for guns and rockets.","The Wodeyars made Mysore Dasara a grand royal festival with an elephant procession and patronised Carnatic music and Kannada literature; Tipu's court used Persian. The Mysore painting style, with gold leaf, developed under the Wodeyars."),
"east-india-company-rule":("Under Company rule, peasants faced heavy revenue demands through the Permanent Settlement, Ryotwari and Mahalwari systems, and many lost land to moneylenders. Bengal's famous weavers were forced to sell cloth cheaply to Company agents, and later British machine-made textiles ruined them. Famines such as Bengal's in 1770 killed millions.","British orientalists founded the Asiatic Society (1784), translated Sanskrit texts and promoted study of India's past, while Christian missionaries and reformers like Ram Mohan Roy debated sati, caste and education. Macaulay's 1835 Minute pushed English education, creating a new educated class."),
"british-raj":("Most Indians remained poor farmers; famines in 1876–78, 1896–1902 and 1943 killed tens of millions. Railways and ports moved raw cotton, jute and wheat to Britain, while new industrial cities like Bombay and Calcutta grew with textile mills and slums. A Western-educated middle class of lawyers, teachers and clerks emerged and led the national movement.","The Raj saw the Bengal Renaissance, social reform movements (Brahmo Samaj, Arya Samaj, Aligarh movement), and literature in Indian languages from Tagore and Premchand. The national movement developed its own culture of khadi, songs like 'Vande Mataram', and mass protest."),
}
